WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.833 --> 00:00:11.132
So, hello I'm Erik Jorgensen from the University of Utah.

00:00:11.133 --> 00:00:14.066
And today we have Drs. Tom Reese and John Heuser,

00:00:14.067 --> 00:00:17.666
and we're going to be interviewing them and discussing

00:00:17.667 --> 00:00:20.599
some of their seminal work that they did in the 1970s.

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:24.066
They were using electron microscopes, which you see in the background,

00:00:24.067 --> 00:00:30.599
this is a device that uses electrons to image cells at a resolution

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.566
close to 1 nanometer. So this is the highest resolution microscope

00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:39.532
possible. In the 1970s they performed experiments that allowed us

00:00:39.533 --> 00:00:43.566
to understand neurotransmission, the fundamental behavior of a

00:00:43.567 --> 00:00:48.232
cell during communication, and they're going to tell us about some of

00:00:48.233 --> 00:00:52.399
their experiments that they performed then. So, what was the

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.366
experiment, what was known, what was not known,

00:00:54.367 --> 00:00:56.466
and what was the experiment and what did it demonstrate?

00:00:56.467 --> 00:01:00.866
We were interested in a little structure in the brain called a

00:01:00.867 --> 00:01:05.532
synapse. And a synapse to me is about the most important

00:01:05.533 --> 00:01:09.399
structure in the universe, because it's the way the nerve cells

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.899
communicate with each other. and it's these networks of

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:17.566
communication that allow us to think and remember. And

00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:23.966
be conscious, and be who we are. So the synapse is sort of

00:01:23.967 --> 00:01:29.266
the rockstar of biology because of its tremendous interest

00:01:29.267 --> 00:01:34.866
and importance. I'd been interested in the synapse

00:01:34.867 --> 00:01:37.166
when there was a great deal already known about it,

00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:40.266
about how it worked electrically, it was known that it

00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:47.466
released drugs, but nobody really had an intimate understanding

00:01:47.467 --> 00:01:54.432
of how an actual individual synapse works. And that's what we wanted.

00:01:54.433 --> 00:02:00.766
Yeah, and I was equally interested. And graduating from medical

00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:06.732
school. And made a beeline for the laboratory where the original

00:02:06.733 --> 00:02:11.166
work had been done that set the stage, where electrophysiologists

00:02:11.167 --> 00:02:14.966
had demonstrated that the communication event at the synapse

00:02:14.967 --> 00:02:19.866
involved release of little packages of chemical transmitters.

00:02:19.867 --> 00:02:24.499
In little electrical blips that they could see. Now the man that did

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:28.799
that in his lab I was joining, Sir Bernard Katz, went on to get the Nobel

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.299
Prize after that. But his insight was profound and on top of that,

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:36.666
he'd written unbelievably beautiful papers that terribly impressed

00:02:36.667 --> 00:02:41.932
me as a student. So made a beeline for that lab. He had

00:02:41.933 --> 00:02:45.932
already looked at electron micrographs and seen that these

00:02:45.933 --> 00:02:50.866
synapses on the inside were filled with tiny membranous

00:02:50.867 --> 00:02:55.232
membrane vesicles, little spheres of membrane. And

00:02:55.233 --> 00:02:59.799
he made the brilliant, but logical, interpretation that those

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:03.666
little spheres contained the packages of transmitters that he was

00:03:03.667 --> 00:03:07.632
measuring electrically as little blips. And so he had the insight to

00:03:07.633 --> 00:03:11.432
propose that the little electrical blips were being generated

00:03:11.433 --> 00:03:16.566
by chemicals coming from those tiny packages. That was established,

00:03:16.567 --> 00:03:19.799
I mean it was such a clear cut idea that it almost had to be

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:24.832
that way. And so my project when I entered that lab as a postdoc

00:03:24.833 --> 00:03:28.599
in those days, you're basically assigned a project, was

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:32.199
to quote "prove it." Get an image of these little vesicles

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:35.599
popping on the surface of the nerve when I stimulate the

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:40.132
nerve. And during that postdoc, I didn't get it. But I had the

00:03:40.133 --> 00:03:44.232
opportunity to join Tom Reese at the NIH immediately after that.

00:03:44.233 --> 00:03:47.966
We reformulated the problem and came up with a whole new

00:03:47.967 --> 00:03:52.799
approach to either proving or disproving that idea that

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:53.599
Katz had had.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:57.799
So Tom, maybe you could tell us how it came to be that you

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:02.132
met John and how that led to a collaboration.

00:04:02.133 --> 00:04:06.432
When John came to the lab, we decided to try a whole different

00:04:06.433 --> 00:04:09.532
approach. As John pointed out, you usually went to a lab

00:04:09.533 --> 00:04:12.732
as a postdoc and were assigned a project, but in his case,

00:04:12.733 --> 00:04:17.499
he came to the lab and assigned me a project.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:22.732
We were originally thinking about looking at the aftermath and trying

00:04:22.733 --> 00:04:27.132
to interpret back to what might've happened. And that

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:29.399
was a good approach.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:34.066
As it turned, yes. And the idea is if these little packages

00:04:34.067 --> 00:04:36.832
are opening to discharge transmitter, and that's the mechanism

00:04:36.833 --> 00:04:41.832
of communication, then during that moment of opening, they should be

00:04:41.833 --> 00:04:44.732
able to capture material from outside the cell and carry it back in.

00:04:44.733 --> 00:04:47.966
And it would be indirect proof that at least they had interacted with

00:04:47.967 --> 00:04:48.599
the surface.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:51.866
So what you demonstrated was that the membrane, the synaptic

00:04:51.867 --> 00:04:55.299
vesicles were normally found in the synapse, in the

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:59.166
bouton, and that they had visited the surface, that's all you

00:04:59.167 --> 00:05:02.732
could show and the problem was a question of time scale or

00:05:02.733 --> 00:05:04.399
was there something missing?

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:09.466
There was the missing fact that we had nothing but

00:05:09.467 --> 00:05:12.199
embalming fluid, and we were dropping these things into

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:15.932
embalming fluid every time. And life did not continue

00:05:15.933 --> 00:05:19.632
in any kind of appropriate manner in that solution.

00:05:19.633 --> 00:05:21.299
So we didn't capture it.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:24.232
There's a missing piece here, and the next step of that,

00:05:24.233 --> 00:05:28.166
was when we decided, well why don't we just stimulate

00:05:28.167 --> 00:05:34.332
the nerve for various periods of time and then fix it

00:05:34.333 --> 00:05:37.332
immediately or let it sit for a while and have a look.

00:05:37.333 --> 00:05:42.132
So we did a series of different times and different amounts of

00:05:42.133 --> 00:05:45.066
stimulation, and different time intervals for recovery. And

00:05:45.067 --> 00:05:50.199
that turned out to be very informative, because when you

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:55.599
stimulated the nerve, the nerve ending where all the vesicles were,

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:59.766
it got quite a bit bigger as if the vesicles inside were being

00:05:59.767 --> 00:06:04.799
added to the surface. And so that was a second piece of

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:09.832
evidence. And then if you waited a while after you stimulated

00:06:09.833 --> 00:06:13.432
before you fixed, you went back to a perfectly normal looking

00:06:13.433 --> 00:06:21.766
terminal. As if there was a cycle of activity and recovery.

00:06:21.767 --> 00:06:26.566
And that cycle, now we know that that cycle involved

00:06:26.567 --> 00:06:31.632
some recycling in the vesicle compartment. And I have to say, John,

00:06:31.633 --> 00:06:36.166
it's always been very clear to me that the idea of vesicle recycling

00:06:36.167 --> 00:06:39.966
was totally yours, okay?

00:06:39.967 --> 00:06:41.932
I wish. -- No, I mean really!

00:06:41.933 --> 00:06:48.466
At the time, I was astonished by this idea because it had never

00:06:48.467 --> 00:06:53.566
occurred to me. And I'll take some credit later for some other stuff.

00:06:53.567 --> 00:06:58.299
Well, it is true that the very first paper that we wrote on the

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:00.932
attempts in London to get it working, we didn't. But we got

00:07:00.933 --> 00:07:06.599
this aftermath effect. We said maybe the vesicles are

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:08.866
dissolving and being reformed or --

00:07:08.867 --> 00:07:11.032
You sort of had that in your head.

00:07:11.033 --> 00:07:16.299
Or maybe it's merely recycling. Merely recycling.

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:19.732
Is the final sentence of my paper in London.

00:07:19.733 --> 00:07:24.732
Merely. It became our career to pinning that down and

00:07:24.733 --> 00:07:25.266
work it out.

00:07:25.267 --> 00:07:33.666
So, back in 1973, one, when you published this, was it accepted at

00:07:33.667 --> 00:07:37.132
that point? Was there anybody else who was doing these experiments

00:07:37.133 --> 00:07:40.299
at the time? Tell me a little bit about what was going on

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:44.132
in 1973 when this paper came out.

00:07:44.133 --> 00:07:50.799
73, there was a whole cadre of people doing this kind of work

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:54.832
all fascinated by the nervous system, all having electron

00:07:54.833 --> 00:07:59.766
microscopy as their tool, they'd become experts at it. That was really

00:07:59.767 --> 00:08:04.966
the heyday of electron microscopy. It was the opportunity

00:08:04.967 --> 00:08:08.332
to see things for the first time at that resolution.

00:08:08.333 --> 00:08:11.766
So everything was a gold mine and everything was fascinating and exciting.

00:08:11.767 --> 00:08:14.799
People were going crazy. So there were a number of people

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:18.532
that were neurobiologists that also focused on the nerve terminal.

00:08:18.533 --> 00:08:23.332
And the remarkable thing was that 2-3 groups, including groups

00:08:23.333 --> 00:08:27.799
here at the Marine Biology Labs and in Europe, particularly in

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:31.332
Milan, were focusing on the exact same preparations as

00:08:31.333 --> 00:08:34.632
us. And doing the exact same experiments. And we were

00:08:34.633 --> 00:08:38.966
publishing in the exact same journals pictures that could've

00:08:38.967 --> 00:08:41.899
been swapped from one paper to the other.

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:45.566
And just because of human nature, they were drawing completely

00:08:45.567 --> 00:08:48.799
different conclusions about how to interpret those

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:52.699
micrographs. So there has never been peace in this field,

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:58.066
there's never been consensus. And we are still, to this day,

00:08:58.067 --> 00:09:01.532
battling out the interpretation of what you see in an electron

00:09:01.533 --> 00:09:05.432
micrograph. But the way we were thinking was indicative of

00:09:05.433 --> 00:09:09.666
the way it's always been. We were -- why we left everybody

00:09:09.667 --> 00:09:12.632
in the dust right then was because we were saying

00:09:12.633 --> 00:09:15.132
how do you see it? How are we going to see that?

00:09:15.133 --> 00:09:17.566
And so we do this, we do this, we put it on that

00:09:17.567 --> 00:09:20.466
substrate, we look at it on this instrument, this and that, etc ...

00:09:20.467 --> 00:09:23.966
We'd move instantly into the technology of it.

00:09:23.967 --> 00:09:30.232
And getting the right image in the period. But you can't forget the rationale

00:09:30.233 --> 00:09:33.366
for doing it at all, for working and developing this machine

00:09:33.367 --> 00:09:38.232
was basically to capture this event of neurotransmission.

00:09:38.233 --> 00:09:42.832
And the physiologists had taught us the event occurs on

00:09:42.833 --> 00:09:48.366
1/1000 of a second. From when activity spreads into the nerve and

00:09:48.367 --> 00:09:51.299
the nerve communicates with the next one by secreting these

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:55.699
little chemicals from synaptic vesicles. It takes only 1/1000

00:09:55.700 --> 00:10:00.566
of a second for it to happen. And we needed to capture that event

00:10:00.567 --> 00:10:04.099
on that same time scale. We needed to capture it on

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:08.566
its own, so that's why we went through on the effort to

00:10:08.567 --> 00:10:09.999
develop the freezing machine further.

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:13.366
So maybe John, you could describe the instrument for

00:10:13.367 --> 00:10:16.732
our viewers and all its glory and complications?

00:10:16.733 --> 00:10:21.299
Well, I wish it were glorious and complicated.

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:27.266
But it couldn't be more trivial. It's a copper block, copper

00:10:27.267 --> 00:10:31.532
chosen because it's very highly conductive metal, the most

00:10:31.533 --> 00:10:34.832
conductive, particularly at those temperatures. So you have a heat

00:10:34.833 --> 00:10:38.899
sink, the best heat sink you can think of or imagine, and

00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:42.499
you want to apply the thing you want to study in the electron microscope,

00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:45.132
in our case these little nerve terminals doing their secretory

00:10:45.133 --> 00:10:51.732
event. Want to apply that prep to the copper block as abruptly

00:10:51.733 --> 00:10:57.099
and precisely timed as possible. So we just plain plunked it down

00:10:57.100 --> 00:11:01.366
on top of it. Just popped it into it. Touched it to the surface.

00:11:01.367 --> 00:11:05.032
To the copper surface at 4 degrees Kelvin. And hook up all

00:11:05.033 --> 00:11:08.699
the stimulating electrodes and develop methods of synchronizing them.

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:12.999
And how was it done? How did you time when you would

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:14.866
stimulate before it hit the copper block?

00:11:14.867 --> 00:11:20.299
Simply had a magnet mounted right next to the sample,

00:11:20.300 --> 00:11:24.732
which could be used by a little sensor that sensing magnets.

00:11:24.733 --> 00:11:29.732
As the magnet passed the sensor, it said, "You're about to hit the

00:11:29.733 --> 00:11:33.632
copper block!" And that stimulated the electrode that

00:11:33.633 --> 00:11:36.699
fired the action potential in the nerve. So we could synchronize

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:40.132
that action potential arrived, the vesicle's discharged,

00:11:40.133 --> 00:11:43.266
and the sample hit the copper block in 1/1000 of a second.

00:11:43.267 --> 00:11:47.132
But you also had a problem with the sample freezing before

00:11:47.133 --> 00:11:48.566
it ever hit the surface.

00:11:48.567 --> 00:11:53.199
It got so close to the copper that it pre-froze before it would

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:58.066
get stimulated. But the real trick was and still is, and you need to use it,

00:11:58.067 --> 00:12:04.899
the drug. The drug that augmented release, our physiology

00:12:04.900 --> 00:12:08.132
friends who helped us work on it said, either between 1000

00:12:08.133 --> 00:12:12.766
or 10,000 times, one nerve impulse would release 10,000 times

00:12:12.767 --> 00:12:17.366
more vesicles than normal. If you make a nerve stimulate

00:12:17.367 --> 00:12:21.566
-- release 10,000 times more than normal, it isn't going to want to do that

00:12:21.567 --> 00:12:25.832
very often. It does it once. And it's shot. So we have to make sure

00:12:25.833 --> 00:12:28.866
we don't pre-stimulate it as we're getting the whole experiment

00:12:28.867 --> 00:12:30.232
ready.

00:12:30.233 --> 00:12:34.732
So, you do the experiment, what do you see? Was there a eureka

00:12:34.733 --> 00:12:38.732
moment where you were on the scope? And you said,

00:12:38.733 --> 00:12:42.399
oh my god, this is it! We've seen the light!

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:44.999
This is the image we were looking for. I mean, can you

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:48.066
describe a little bit about that? Were you together, where did it happen?

00:12:48.067 --> 00:12:51.366
As it happened, Tom wasn't there at the particular time that it happened.

00:12:51.367 --> 00:12:54.966
He was back at NIH, and I was in San Francisco. But at that time,

00:12:54.967 --> 00:12:59.132
I had Mike Denitz was a very fine physiologist who was doing

00:12:59.133 --> 00:13:02.399
all this treatment with this magical drug that increased

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:08.666
things so much. And we had two young, even pre-docs,

00:13:08.667 --> 00:13:10.932
maybe they had just gotten their postdoc, and they

00:13:10.933 --> 00:13:13.899
wanted to learn some EM. So they were wandering around

00:13:13.900 --> 00:13:18.832
my lab getting in the way underfoot, but one of them,

00:13:18.833 --> 00:13:22.966
Lily Jan and her husband Yuh-Nung Jan, was the other

00:13:22.967 --> 00:13:25.966
person wandering around. Both have gotten on to great careers.

00:13:25.967 --> 00:13:30.266
But they, so they're sitting on the microscope, and they called me

00:13:30.267 --> 00:13:34.099
into the microscope, and they said, John, I think we've got it.

00:13:34.100 --> 00:13:36.532
I mean, look at that. And I came in and looked over their

00:13:36.533 --> 00:13:39.299
shoulder and I said, no, no, no. That's a capillary.

00:13:39.300 --> 00:13:42.766
You don't recognize there are whole bunch of little vesicles

00:13:42.767 --> 00:13:45.199
attached to the surface of the capillary all the time.

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:48.632
That's part of transport of blood. Nothing to do with

00:13:48.633 --> 00:13:52.799
nerves. You know, forget it. Looked a little harder, my god,

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:56.932
it is a nerve terminal! Look at all those openings. That really was a

00:13:56.933 --> 00:13:58.166
eureka moment.

00:13:58.167 --> 00:14:00.632
What was it you saw? What was it that you were actually

00:14:00.633 --> 00:14:03.266
looking for that was the eureka?

00:14:03.267 --> 00:14:06.166
Tom had explained how at that point were doing it by freeze

00:14:06.167 --> 00:14:08.832
fracture. It's a method of looking at the surface of the

00:14:08.833 --> 00:14:12.699
nerve by cracking open this frozen tissue. Particularly

00:14:12.700 --> 00:14:14.732
nice, because after all, we'd started by freezing it.

00:14:14.733 --> 00:14:16.632
So why not take advantage of that new technology.

00:14:16.633 --> 00:14:20.232
So we're cracking all these nerves open. It's this tube,

00:14:20.233 --> 00:14:24.666
so here's a long cylinder. And on that cylinder, dotting

00:14:24.667 --> 00:14:29.466
all the way down are these small craters. Like craters on the moon.

00:14:29.467 --> 00:14:32.599
And it's a very small jump to --

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:37.132
Remember the vesicles were originally lined up on this membrane.

00:14:37.133 --> 00:14:40.432
Inside, and you couldn't see it from the outside.

00:14:40.433 --> 00:14:43.032
You wouldn't have seen them at all. And so the fact that

00:14:43.033 --> 00:14:47.266
you could now see openings, it meant that they had actually

00:14:47.267 --> 00:14:53.732
exploded open under the surface of the -- just right before

00:14:53.733 --> 00:14:59.866
the muscle hit the copper block and froze. So we'd captured

00:14:59.867 --> 00:15:07.299
them in millisecond timescale. I don't think anybody had ever done

00:15:07.300 --> 00:15:11.766
any anatomy structures of things at that time scale.

00:15:11.767 --> 00:15:13.899
There was another story that emerged right after that, and that is

00:15:13.900 --> 00:15:19.199
after the vesicle opened, we noticed that in the wall

00:15:19.200 --> 00:15:22.332
-- in the membrane of the vesicle, there were various kinds of

00:15:22.333 --> 00:15:26.066
structures and that as time -- you see it with this freezing,

00:15:26.067 --> 00:15:30.566
you do a time experiment. You can stimulate at 10ms, 20ms,

00:15:30.567 --> 00:15:34.899
30ms, so we noticed that as time passed, that the vesicle

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:38.566
was getting wider and wider open, and that some of these

00:15:38.567 --> 00:15:41.732
particles inside, these structures inside, were coming out

00:15:41.733 --> 00:15:45.232
and fusing into the surface membrane. And so that was now

00:15:45.233 --> 00:15:48.066
the story where we measured all that. And that story,

00:15:48.067 --> 00:15:53.832
we made a story out of that. So that was a third leg.

00:15:53.833 --> 00:15:56.732
That was actually -- I enjoyed, actually, that part of it.

00:15:56.733 --> 00:16:00.299
Well, it's a critical piece of the puzzle. Because if you have

00:16:00.300 --> 00:16:02.832
these little packages of transmitter and they're capable of

00:16:02.833 --> 00:16:06.932
fusing with the surface in 1/1000 of a second, activated

00:16:06.933 --> 00:16:10.899
by calcium, what happens to them after that? And why

00:16:10.900 --> 00:16:13.866
were we seeing all these strange changes at much longer

00:16:13.867 --> 00:16:19.232
times? Well, that's when we really had fun with the freezing machine.

00:16:19.233 --> 00:16:22.866
We were no longer just trying to capture the immediate moment

00:16:22.867 --> 00:16:26.399
but the aftermath of the event. As Tom described it, we

00:16:26.400 --> 00:16:29.932
could now do aftermath in really close coordination. We could do

00:16:29.933 --> 00:16:34.999
looking just milliseconds later, and it's our conviction that

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:40.132
it's in the nature of the tiny membrane vesicles, something about

00:16:40.133 --> 00:16:44.899
biophysics of membranes, that when that joins a larger membrane,

00:16:44.900 --> 00:16:49.766
like the surface of the nerve terminal, it has no alternative

00:16:49.767 --> 00:16:53.766
but it has to collapse. It has to flatten out, join that

00:16:53.767 --> 00:16:57.632
membrane completely. Therefore, deposit all of its

00:16:57.633 --> 00:17:00.666
contents and its whole nature.

00:17:00.667 --> 00:17:03.532
Did this end the debate? So you showed the vesicle touches,

00:17:03.533 --> 00:17:07.299
flattens out, and you basically, it's irreversible.

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:11.132
Absolutely. It ended the debate. Erik, you're baiting us because

00:17:11.133 --> 00:17:17.632
you know perfectly well that our colleagues in Italy,

00:17:17.633 --> 00:17:20.632
our colleagues at the MBL and such, did not accept

00:17:20.633 --> 00:17:25.299
that this was happening. Yeah, they had to accept

00:17:25.300 --> 00:17:30.899
that Katz was fundamentally right, that's how neurotransmission

00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:35.532
occurs, by the fusion of these vesicles. But then is when interpretation

00:17:35.533 --> 00:17:39.366
of electron micrographs end and how you did the experiment,

00:17:39.367 --> 00:17:45.732
because they looked at the same micrographs of a vesicle

00:17:45.733 --> 00:17:50.866
fused with a cell surface and concluded that that was it.

00:17:50.867 --> 00:17:55.199
That that vesicle did that transiently, a little kiss. Kissed the

00:17:55.200 --> 00:17:58.832
surface, let out transmitter, and simply reversed the process.

00:17:58.833 --> 00:18:01.732
Now I was earlier describing to you that we thought

00:18:01.733 --> 00:18:05.199
it's in the biophysics in membranes that they can't do that.

00:18:05.200 --> 00:18:08.366
That if they've joined another bigger guy, they're going to merge

00:18:08.367 --> 00:18:12.532
with it completely. And so there's a controversy that

00:18:12.533 --> 00:18:16.732
is embedded in the literature, in scientific thinking

00:18:16.733 --> 00:18:20.899
to date. And everybody asks about this still, are you

00:18:20.900 --> 00:18:25.832
really correct that all of your aftermath stuff is really

00:18:25.833 --> 00:18:30.799
true? Or should we believe the Italian group that it's just

00:18:30.800 --> 00:18:33.332
a kiss? The vesicles are kissing and coming right back in

00:18:33.333 --> 00:18:38.066
again and again, in exact momentary exposure and

00:18:38.067 --> 00:18:41.499
enclosure. And that's the true biophysics.

00:18:41.500 --> 00:18:45.932
And so you then did experiments that were over longer

00:18:45.933 --> 00:18:48.932
and longer periods of time. And then you saw something

00:18:48.933 --> 00:18:52.866
that said, well there is evidence that in fact these

00:18:52.867 --> 00:18:55.699
vesicles are collapsing fully into the membrane.

00:18:55.700 --> 00:18:59.166
Yes, the aftermath experiments of milliseconds.

00:18:59.167 --> 00:19:03.866
Particles inside the membrane, the fusing into the membrane.

00:19:03.867 --> 00:19:05.632
Particles being our marker, and membrane.

00:19:05.633 --> 00:19:09.899
Particles are places where the membrane is impinged by

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:14.499
a transmembrane protein, and freeze fraction will show up as bumps.

00:19:14.500 --> 00:19:20.032
And the bumps were diffusing out of the vesicle membrane

00:19:20.033 --> 00:19:22.866
into the whole other membrane. Now it's not going to do that

00:19:22.867 --> 00:19:24.766
in a kiss and run.

00:19:24.767 --> 00:19:27.899
What is it that you know, you saw in your images that

00:19:27.900 --> 00:19:31.399
convinced you that vesicles had to be recycled?

00:19:31.400 --> 00:19:33.232
You know, the reason that I would've had a preliminary,

00:19:33.233 --> 00:19:37.366
that I had a preliminary glimpse of that and said that maybe

00:19:37.367 --> 00:19:40.866
merely recycling happened was because of the magic

00:19:40.867 --> 00:19:44.599
clathrin coated vesicles that appeared in the nerve

00:19:44.600 --> 00:19:48.899
terminal as a consequence of stimulation. And I had just

00:19:48.900 --> 00:19:53.199
come from my undergraduate work, where I was exposed

00:19:53.200 --> 00:19:57.632
to the father of electron microscopy, Keith Porter, and

00:19:57.633 --> 00:20:01.232
his -- one of his, he had trained so many outstanding

00:20:01.233 --> 00:20:04.332
people, but the person who was still present when I was in

00:20:04.333 --> 00:20:08.966
college, was Tom Roth. And he had just discovered that

00:20:08.967 --> 00:20:12.499
the mechanism for endocytosis, the mechanism of uptake of material

00:20:12.500 --> 00:20:17.266
from the outside world in all cells is mediated by a vesicle

00:20:17.267 --> 00:20:21.299
that has a coat, a beautiful cytoplasmic coat that actually is

00:20:21.300 --> 00:20:25.399
polygonal. Like a geodesic dome. And you can see that

00:20:25.400 --> 00:20:29.032
dome in the electron microscope. And so here I'm the young guy

00:20:29.033 --> 00:20:34.766
in London, and I see these domes in nerve terminals as a

00:20:34.767 --> 00:20:35.999
consequence of stimulation.

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:37.132
You'd seen those in London?

00:20:37.133 --> 00:20:38.099
Yeah.

00:20:38.100 --> 00:20:38.999
Oh, I didn't know that.

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:41.532
And I said, oh my god, it's endocytosis!

00:20:41.533 --> 00:20:46.532
And then it's also interesting that we managed to end a

00:20:46.533 --> 00:20:50.632
total contentious situation with our Italian competitors

00:20:50.633 --> 00:20:55.632
over that. Because they couldn't see these coated vesicles.

00:20:55.633 --> 00:21:00.299
And why couldn't they see it and we did? Well, it was simply

00:21:00.300 --> 00:21:03.432
that technique of embalming that we used. Our was a little bit

00:21:03.433 --> 00:21:06.999
different than theirs. Ours had a little bit more heavy metal

00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:11.066
than theirs. So, the coats looked a little more dramatic in our pictures

00:21:11.067 --> 00:21:14.566
than in theirs. But that's the way that electron microscopists

00:21:14.567 --> 00:21:20.599
butt horns and can enter total disagreement. Total contentions

00:21:20.600 --> 00:21:21.666
over this kind of issue.

00:21:21.667 --> 00:21:27.532
In the end, what I think solved the debate, are those freeze

00:21:27.533 --> 00:21:30.532
etched images, where you see the clathrin coats.

00:21:30.533 --> 00:21:34.399
This is absolutely clear that geodesic structures, absolutely

00:21:34.400 --> 00:21:37.199
clear. Maybe you could tell us a little bit about the invention

00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:40.066
of the freeze etch technique and how you used that to show

00:21:40.067 --> 00:21:43.132
that there were clathrin coated vesicles?

00:21:43.133 --> 00:21:46.499
That was a late chapter that arose from being able to

00:21:46.500 --> 00:21:52.199
directly freeze things. Because if you freeze things out

00:21:52.200 --> 00:21:56.766
of their natural juice, when you try to draw the water away

00:21:56.767 --> 00:22:00.532
the contents of the juice collapse down on top of everything,

00:22:00.533 --> 00:22:02.099
you can't see anything. So something we were able to

00:22:02.100 --> 00:22:06.732
do was freeze directly and not using a fixative, which also

00:22:06.733 --> 00:22:14.199
destroys things. We had a whole world of looking at the inside

00:22:14.200 --> 00:22:17.799
of membranes. And that was invented in our summer neurobiology

00:22:17.800 --> 00:22:22.066
course at Woods Hole by one of John's students.

00:22:22.067 --> 00:22:24.432
They made a mistake on one of the machines and

00:22:24.433 --> 00:22:29.399
John decided to have a look at it anyway, and that was one

00:22:29.400 --> 00:22:33.432
of those eureka moments. All of a sudden, for the first time

00:22:33.433 --> 00:22:37.132
we could actually look inside the cytoplasm. Not just look

00:22:37.133 --> 00:22:40.266
at the membranes like we do with freeze fraction.

00:22:40.267 --> 00:22:44.599
We could look inside the cytoplasm and you could see all

00:22:44.600 --> 00:22:51.299
these wonderful coats. And John went on back to San Francisco

00:22:51.300 --> 00:22:58.932
and began a decade of marvelous work looking at the

00:22:58.933 --> 00:23:03.066
coated vesicle and coated vesicle endocytosis and cycling.

00:23:03.067 --> 00:23:06.699
I think that's a good place to end it, but before we do,

00:23:06.700 --> 00:23:10.399
I would like to ask if whether you guys have anything

00:23:10.400 --> 00:23:13.499
like reflections, or memories or anything else you would like to add?

00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:19.099
Thank you for being my friend all these years. In spite of

00:23:19.100 --> 00:23:20.732
a few differences we have every now and then.

00:23:20.733 --> 00:23:24.999
I can say exactly the same thing. Thank you for being my

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:32.266
friend. And muse, and a colleague, and it's just like.

00:23:32.267 --> 00:23:52.167
I don't know. Hahahaha.

